    "result_text": "Done. The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test run, 0 failures).\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe old `AssignMembershipLevel` hardcoded a cap of 3 with a comment saying \"level 4 is reserved for tenure\" \u2014 but tenure was never actually defined anywhere. I made that rule real and put it where it belongs.\n\n**New domain helper** \u2014 `model/MembershipLevel.java` (alongside the existing pure helpers `AgeBand`, `Luhn`, `E164`, etc.):\n- Level starts at 1 and gains a point for each loyalty factor: has an email, had no namesakes at creation (`namesakeCount == 0`), and **tenure greater than 365 days** (`TENURE_DAYS_FOR_TOP_LEVEL`).\n- The two creation-time factors alone saturate at level 3; the tenure factor is the only route to level 4. This encodes \"level 4 requires tenure > 365 days\" as a genuine necessary condition rather than a comment.\n\n**Refactored function** \u2014 `AssignMembershipLevel.java`:\n- Now delegates to `MembershipLevel.forOwner(...)`, passing `TENURE_AT_CREATION = 0` since a newly created owner has accrued no tenure.\n- Because tenure is 0 at creation, the tenure factor never fires, so a new owner never exceeds level 3 \u2014 e.g. email + `namesakeCount` 0 lands at exactly 3, matching the acceptance test.\n\nBehaviour for every create-owner path is identical to before (the tenure branch is dead at creation), but the \"what makes level 4\" rule is now defined in one small, single-responsibility unit and reusable if tenure is ever evaluated after creation. `namesakeCount` null-safety is preserved inside the helper.",
